	<summary type="text">An ancient undead monster terrorizes Victorian London.</summary>
	<content type="html">&lt;p&gt;&lt;i&gt;Dracula&lt;/i&gt; is one of the most famous horror novels, responsible not just for introducing the eponymous Count Dracula, but for laying the foundations for many of the common tropes we see in modern horror fiction.&lt;/p&gt;
			&lt;p&gt;Count Dracula isn’t the first vampire to have graced the pages of Western literature—that honor belongs to Lord Ruthven in “&lt;a href=&quot;https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/john-william-polidori/the-vampire&quot;&gt;The Vampire&lt;/a&gt;,” a short story by &lt;a href=&quot;https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/john-william-polidori&quot;&gt;John William Polidori&lt;/a&gt;—but Dracula is the vampire on which the modern vampire trope is based.&lt;/p&gt;
			&lt;p&gt;&lt;i&gt;Dracula&lt;/i&gt; wasn’t as famous in its day as it is today; readers of the time seemed to enjoy it as nothing more than a good story, and &lt;a href=&quot;https://standardebooks.org/ebooks/bram-stoker&quot;&gt;Stoker&lt;/a&gt; died nearly penniless. But its long-lasting influence is undeniable, and for all its age &lt;i&gt;Dracula&lt;/i&gt; remains a gripping, fast-paced, and enjoyable read.&lt;/p&gt;</content>
